\section*{HAVING vs WHERE in SQL}

In SQL, both the \texttt{HAVING} and \texttt{WHERE} clauses are used to filter data, but they differ in terms of \textbf{when} and \textbf{how} they are applied:

\subsection*{1. WHERE Clause}
\textbf{Purpose}: Filters rows \textbf{before} any grouping or aggregation (such as \texttt{GROUP BY}) is done.\\
\textbf{Use}: Used to filter rows based on conditions on individual rows or columns.

\begin{verbatim}
SELECT employee_id, salary
FROM employees
WHERE salary > 50000;
\end{verbatim}

In this example, only employees with a salary greater than 50,000 will be returned. The filtering is done \textbf{before} the result set is aggregated (if there's any aggregation).

\subsection*{2. HAVING Clause}
\textbf{Purpose}: Filters groups \textbf{after} the aggregation process (like \texttt{GROUP BY}).\\
\textbf{Use}: Used to filter results based on aggregate functions (such as \texttt{SUM}, \texttt{COUNT}, \texttt{AVG}, etc.).

\begin{verbatim}
SELECT department_id, AVG(salary) AS avg_salary
FROM employees
GROUP BY department_id
HAVING AVG(salary) > 60000;
\end{verbatim}

In this case, the query groups the employees by \texttt{department\_id} and calculates the average salary for each group. The \texttt{HAVING} clause then filters out departments whose average salary is \textbf{not} greater than 60,000. This filtering happens \textbf{after} the aggregation.

\subsection*{Key Differences}

\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}
\hline
\textbf{Feature} & \textbf{WHERE} & \textbf{HAVING} \\
\hline
\textbf{When applied} & Before grouping/aggregation & After grouping/aggregation \\
\textbf{Used for} & Filtering rows & Filtering groups \\
\textbf{Can filter by} & Column values and conditions & Aggregate functions and conditions \\
\hline
\end{tabular}

\subsection*{Use Case Summary}
- Use \texttt{WHERE} to filter \textbf{individual rows} before any grouping.
- Use \texttt{HAVING} to filter \textbf{groups or aggregated data} after the grouping process is complete.

